{"title":"Abstract","markdown":{"headingText":"Abstract","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\nBat activity reflects seasonal phenology and nightly environmental conditions that frequently covary, making their contributions difficult to distinguish from marginal associations alone. We investigated these relationships at eight sites spanning 581–1,846 m elevation in the northern Sierra Nevada of California from April to October 2015. After excluding site-nights without usable acoustic recordings, the analytical dataset contained 1,056 site-nights and 17 bat species. We fitted Bayesian negative-binomial generalized additive models that represented site-specific seasonal phenology and nonlinear associations with temperature, relative humidity, and lunar phase. We also grouped species by characteristic echolocation frequency into low- (\\<25 kHz), mid- (25–40 kHz), and high-frequency (\\>40 kHz) groups to test an a priori prediction from acoustic-attenuation theory that sensitivity to atmospheric conditions would increase with call frequency. Seasonal activity differed substantially among sites, and the inferred seasonal trajectory changed markedly after conditioning on nightly environmental conditions. Temperature was positively associated with activity across all three frequency groups. In contrast, the association with relative humidity became increasingly negative from low- to mid- to high-frequency groups, with the strongest decline among high-frequency species, matching the predicted ordering from ultrasonic sound propagation. Lunar responses were weaker and less consistent among frequency groups. These results indicate that environmental conditions can influence bat assemblages through both broadly shared ecological processes and mechanisms linked to sensory biology. In particular, the correspondence between the predicted and observed frequency-dependent humidity response provides field evidence that atmospheric transmission contributes to environmental variation in bat activity.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"docx":{"identifier":{"display-name":"MS Word","target-format":"docx","base-format":"docx"},"execute":{"fig-width":5,"fig-height":4,"fig-format":"png","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":"auto","echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"docx","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":true,"merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"page-width":6.5},"pandoc":{"default-image-extension":"png","to":"docx","toc":false,"number-sections":false,"output-file":"abstract.docx"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website":"On this page","related-formats-title":"Other Formats","related-notebooks-title":"Notebooks","source-notebooks-prefix":"Source","other-links-title":"Other Links","code-links-title":"Code Links","launch-dev-container-title":"Launch Dev Container","launch-binder-title":"Launch Binder","article-notebook-label":"Article Notebook","notebook-preview-download":"Download Notebook","notebook-preview-download-src":"Download Source","notebook-preview-back":"Back to Article","manuscript-meca-bundle":"MECA